@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+# ADR 0002: JSON Patch
+
+- Version: 1
+- Date: 2026-10-22
+- Status: accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Our services need to change JSON documents with standard patches. RFC 6902 defines JSON Patch, a
+sequence of `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test` operations whose locations are JSON
+Pointers, which this library already resolves ([ADR 0001](0001-json-pointer.md)).
+
+## Decisions
+
+1. **Immutable, all-or-nothing application.** `applyPatch` deep-copies the document and applies the
+   operations to the copy, returning it only if every operation succeeds. RFC 6902 section 5 requires a
+   failing patch to have no effect; working on a copy gives that without an undo log, and callers
+   never see their stored records half-changed or later mutated through shared references. Values
+   taken from the patch are copied too, so the result shares nothing with either input.
+2. **Validate the whole patch first.** Malformed operations are reported before any operation runs,
+   so an invalid patch is rejected even when an earlier operation would also have failed; the error
+   then points at the malformed operation, which is the problem the sender must fix.
+3. **JSON values only.** The document and every `value` must be `null`, booleans, finite numbers,
+   strings, arrays or plain objects. Other JavaScript values (`undefined`, `NaN`, dates, class
+   instances) have no JSON meaning, would make `test` and the result ambiguous, and are rejected at
+   this boundary rather than silently dropped or converted.
+4. **Members are written with `Object.defineProperty`.** Assigning `object["__proto__"]` would change
+   the prototype of the object instead of creating a member, a prototype-pollution vector for patches
+   that come from other services. Defining the property keeps `__proto__` an ordinary own member,
+   consistent with the own-properties-only lookup of ADR 0001.
+5. **Removing the whole document is an error.** RFC 6902 does not define a document without a root
+   value, so `remove` with path `""` fails instead of returning `undefined`.
+6. **One error type, `JsonPatchError`,** carrying the index and the operation as given, with the
+   underlying error (often a `JsonPointerError`) as `cause`, so callers can report which operation
+   failed and why while catching patch failures alone.
+7. **Unknown operation members are ignored,** as RFC 6902 section 4 requires, and `op` values are
+   case-sensitive.
